.TH man "" "Command"
.SH NAME
man \- print online manual sections
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBman [\-csnw] \fItitle ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
man -- print online manual sections
Usage:	man [ -csnw ] title ...
Options:
	-c	Look in command section
	-s	Look in system section
	-n	Re-nroff the output
	-w	Print only file name where document resides
.HE
.B man
locates and prints the manual page for each
.I title
named on the command line.
Pages live below
.BR /usr/man .
By default
.B man
searches both the command and the system sections and displays the
pre-formatted copy of the page through
.BR scat .
.PP
The section flags may be combined; when both are given (the default), each
.I title
is looked for in the command section first and then the system section.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Look only in the command section.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Look only in the system section.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Re-format the page: run its unformatted source through
.B nroff \-man
and pipe the result through
.BR scat .
Any option beginning with
.B \-
other than those listed here is passed on to
.B nroff
unchanged.
.IP \fB\-w\fR
Do not display the page; print only the path of the file in which the
document resides.
.PP
If a
.I title
is not found,
.B man
reports whether it was missing from the command section, the system
section, or the manual as a whole.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 24n
\fB/usr/man\fR	root of the manual
\fB/usr/man/cmd\fR	command section, unformatted
\fB/usr/man/sys\fR	system section, unformatted
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR help ,
.BR nroff ,
.B scat
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
With no
.I title
argument,
.B man
prints
.B "try man man"
and exits with a nonzero status.
For each
.I title
not found it prints a message naming the section that was searched.
